# First hints: from a blank file to live help

File: lsp/docs/first_hints.oot

The writing helper watches your files and speaks up: it finishes
names, explains words, and flags mistakes while you type. Your
editor talks to it; you never type at it directly. This page takes
you from nothing to your first live hint.

## What you need

- The helper installed (`ooda-lsp` answers `--version`).
- Any editor that speaks the standard editor-helper protocol.
  There is no bundled openOODA editor add-on; wire a standard one.
- A folder with one `.oo` file in it. That is your workspace.

For every knob and flag, see `client_config.oot` and `lsp_setup.oot`.
This page is the short happy path.

## The five-minute setup

1. Point your editor's helper setting at `ooda-lsp --stdio`.
   Add `--oodar-root <path>` so jump-to-definition works on
   permission words. Add `--stdlib-root <path>` so names from
   the shared tool box complete too.
2. Open your workspace folder in the editor, then open the file.
   The editor shakes hands with the helper on its own.
3. Type the start of a known name and pause. A list of
   completions should appear. That is your first hint.
4. Hover a name. A short explanation should pop up.
5. Break something small on purpose: delete one closing mark.
   A colored flag should appear on that line. Put the mark back
   and the flag clears. The helper is now watching.

## Try it without an editor

One-shot mode runs a single saved request and prints the answer.
Save the request in `examples/initialize_request.oot` to a file,
then run the helper with that file as its argument. The printed
answer carries `serverInfo.version` (matches the `VERSION` file)
and the full list of what the helper can do. The other two
examples show a completion ask and a hover ask in the same style.

## When no hints appear

1. Check the helper runs: `ooda-lsp --version` must print a number.
2. Check the handshake: the editor must send its opening message
   first. Typing-related asks before that get an error, not hints.
3. Check the flags: a `--stdlib-root` or `--oodar-root` that points
   nowhere stops the helper at start. Fix the path.
4. Check the file: hints only fire inside the open workspace,
   on `.oo` files the helper can read.
